# Test fixtures are byte-exact copies of scraped pages. The decode tests assert
# on their SHA-256 and byte size, and several exist precisely to pin down odd
# bytes (CP1252 curly quotes, CRLF line endings, raw UTF-8). Any normalisation
# git applied here would silently change the thing under test.
tests/fixtures/*.htm -text -diff

# The corpus is committed as ordinary git objects, deliberately. It was briefly
# put behind git-lfs; that never took effect, because `pages/` with a trailing
# slash matches no file path and .gitattributes only ever consults file paths.
# Repairing it to `pages/**` would have made things worse, not better:
#
#   plain git, packed   3.5 MiB      (25 MB of near-identical HTML deltas well)
#   git-lfs            ~23 MB        (LFS stores blobs undeltified and
#                                     uncompressed) + 5,557 pointer files
#
# LFS earns its place for large binaries that do not delta. This is 5,557 small,
# highly repetitive text files — the opposite case. Committing them plainly also
# keeps `git diff` and `git log -p` working on the source data, and drops the
# git-lfs prerequisite for anyone cloning.
#
# The corpus must not be line-ending-normalised, for the same reason as the
# fixtures above: parsed records are keyed by the SHA-256 of the source bytes.
pages/*.htm -text
